        "testata": "The Hill",
        "lingua_originale": "en",
        "titolo_orig": "Ethics Committee launches investigation into Swalwell",
        "testo_it": "USA: la Commissione Etica della Camera avvia un'indagine sul deputato Eric Swalwell.",
        "priorita": "Bassa",
        "id_gruppo": 16,
        "link": "https://thehill.com/homenews/house/5829199-ethics-committee-swalwell/",
        "orario": "20:47",
        "timestamp": 1776106069,
        "contenuto_esteso": "House Ethics Committee launches investigation into Swalwell by Mike Lillis - 04/13/26 2:47 PM ET by Mike Lillis - 04/13/26 2:47 PM ET The House Ethics Committee has launched an investigation into Rep. Eric Swalwell (D-Calif.) as he faces allegations of sexual misconduct. The probe follows explosive accusations from a former aide that Swalwell sexually assaulted her on two occasions — charges that were first made public on Friday in the San Francisco Chronicle . That article was followed by a CNN report featuring three other women airing separate allegations of sexual misconduct. Swalwell has denied the allegations. But the reports prompted a number of Democrats to drop their support for Swalwell’s run at the California governor’s mansion — and forced him to quit the race on Sunday night. “To my family, staff, friends, and supporters, I am deeply sorry for mistakes in judgment I’ve made in my past,” Swalwell posted on the social platform X. “I will fight the serious, false allegations that have been made — but that’s my fight, not a campaign’s.” The Ethics Committee’s probe is just the latest fallout from the stunning developments surrounding Swalwell, who was a leading contender to replace Gov. Gavin Newsom (D-Calif.) before the allegations were published. Over the weekend, reports emerged that the Manhattan District Attorney’s Office is also conducting a criminal investigation into the accusations, because one of the assaults was alleged to have taken place in New York. The reverberations are also being felt on Capitol Hill , where Rep. Anna Paulina Luna (R-Fla.) is vowing to move a resolution expelling Swalwell from Congress. The effort is being supported by a small but growing number of Democrats — if Swalwell’s expulsion is accompanied by a separate resolution to eject Rep. Tony Gonzales (R-Texas), who has acknowledged an extramarital affair with a staffer who later died by self-immolation. It’s unclear if House leaders in either party are ready to support the expulsion efforts. But any lawmaker has the power to force so-called privileged resolutions to the floor for a vote, even if it’s not sanctioned by leadership. The fast-moving events could preclude any formal conclusions from the Ethics Committee, which typically takes months or years to conduct a probe. Add as preferred source on Google Copyright 2026 Nexstar Media Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. More House News See All See All Video/Hill.TV See all Hill.TV See all Video See all Hill.TV See all Video Top Stories See All See All"
